# =============================================================================
# Grant Platform – AWS serverless target configuration
# =============================================================================
# Copy to `deploy/aws/.env` and set values. That file is gitignored.
#
#   cp deploy/aws/.env.example deploy/aws/.env
#
# This is the AWS analogue of the Helm chart's `config:` block in
# charts/grant-platform/values.yaml. Every key here is a key of `@grantjs/env`;
# nothing is invented for this target.
#
# Layering, outermost last:
#
#   AWS_TARGET_ENV_DEFAULTS  ->  this file  ->  values the stack computes itself
#
# Keys you leave blank keep their default — copying this file unedited changes
# nothing. See deploy/aws/lib/config/defaults.ts for what the target already picks
# on your behalf (DynamoDB cache, S3 storage, EMF telemetry, Secrets Manager,
# migrations off at boot, and the origin-verify requirement).
#
# SECRETS: the two keys in the last section never enter the CloudFormation
# template. CloudFormation cannot hold a literal secret without it being readable
# by anyone who can describe the stack, so they are written to the platform secret
# out of band after deploy. The application resolves them through ISecretResolver,
# so a change takes effect within the resolver's TTL — no redeploy. (ADR 0004.)
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Identity
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stable UUID for the platform's system user. Generate once and keep it: it owns
# audit rows and the signing key, so changing it later orphans them.
#   uuidgen | tr 'A-Z' 'a-z'
SYSTEM_USER_ID=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Authentication
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Minimum assurance level accepted at login. `aal2` requires a second factor.
#   aal1 | aal2
AUTH_MIN_AAL_AT_LOGIN=

# GitHub OAuth application. The ID is public and belongs here; the secret does not
# — see the secrets section below.
GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
# Must match the callback registered on the OAuth app, on your deployed hostname.
GITHUB_CALLBACK_URL=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Email
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `ses` uses the Lambda's execution role — no access keys to issue or rotate. The
# stack grants ses:SendEmail; the identity must be verified in this region, and an
# account still in the SES sandbox can only send to verified addresses.
#   console | ses | smtp | mailgun | mailjet
EMAIL_PROVIDER=
# Must be an SES-verified identity (address, or any address at a verified domain).
EMAIL_FROM=
EMAIL_FROM_NAME=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scheduled jobs
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave blank. This target defaults to `aws`, and the stack provides both halves the
# adapter needs: six EventBridge rules for the recurring sweeps and an SQS queue for
# one-off work, consumed by a jobs function that has no public endpoint.
#
# `node-cron` — the default everywhere else — schedules timers inside the process, and
# a Lambda execution environment is frozen between invocations and destroyed when idle,
# so those timers fire only while a container happens to be thawed. Selecting it here
# gives you unreliable schedules *and* duplicates of every rule.
#   node-cron | bullmq | aws
JOBS_PROVIDER=

# Turns every job off, including the enqueue path. `startProjectSync` then fails with
# "job adapter is not configured" rather than queueing.
JOBS_ENABLED=

# Individual schedules and switches. The EventBridge rules are generated from these
# same keys, so changing one moves the rule and the application's view of it together.
# Cron is 5-field UTC, as node-cron parses it; the stack translates to EventBridge
# syntax and fails at synth if an expression cannot be expressed there.
#   JOBS_DATA_RETENTION_SCHEDULE=0 2 * * *
#   JOBS_WEBHOOK_DELIVERY_SCHEDULE=* * * * *
#   JOBS_SYSTEM_SIGNING_KEY_ROTATION_ENABLED=true

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rate limiting
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Keyed on the client IP, which on this target comes from `cloudfront-viewer-address`
# — a header CloudFront *overwrites*, so it cannot be spoofed the way X-Forwarded-For
# can. That makes the limit genuinely per-device, which is also why the default bites
# sooner here than it looks: 100 requests per 15 minutes is ~6.7 a minute for an entire
# browser, and a dashboard polling a running sync job exhausts it in minutes.
#
# Raise it rather than disabling it. The Lambda Function URL answers the internet and
# origin verification is enforced *in* the function, so a refused request still costs an
# invocation — this limit is one of the few controls in front of that.
#   SECURITY_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=5000
#   SECURITY_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_MINUTES=15
#
# Auth endpoints have their own, deliberately tighter, limit: it is the
# credential-guessing surface and no real session needs many logins per window.
#   SECURITY_RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX=200
#   SECURITY_RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_WINDOW_MINUTES=15
#
# `SECURITY_ENABLE_RATE_LIMIT=false` turns it off entirely. On an internet-facing
# deployment that is a decision, not a tuning step.
SECURITY_RATE_LIMIT_MAX=
SECURITY_RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_MAX=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Webhooks
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Both of these relax the SSRF guard. Leave blank in anything internet-facing;
# they exist for local targets and for delivering inside a private network.
WEBHOOKS_ALLOW_HTTP=
WEBHOOKS_SSRF_ALLOW_PRIVATE=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Overrides
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Any other @grantjs/env key can be set here and is passed through unchanged —
# into the Lambda environment and therefore into the CloudFormation template, in
# plaintext, readable by anyone who can describe the stack. Only the two keys in the
# secrets section below are routed out of band.
#
# Credential-shaped keys are therefore REFUSED at synth rather than deployed
# insecurely — SMTP_PASSWORD, MAILGUN_API_KEY, MAILJET_API_KEY, MAILJET_SECRET_KEY,
# REDIS_PASSWORD, POSTGRES_PASSWORD, SECURITY_API_KEY, EMAIL_SES_CLIENT_SECRET and the
# *_ACCESS_KEY_ID / *_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY pairs. The application reads those from
# process.env rather than through ISecretResolver, so putting them in the platform
# secret would hide them from the app as well as from the template; there is no safe
# path for them on this target yet. Making them resolver-backed is a follow-on story.
# Gate 4, finding F-C.
# The target's defaults already cover CACHE_STRATEGY, STORAGE_PROVIDER,
# TELEMETRY_PROVIDER, SECRETS_PROVIDER, DB_BOOTSTRAP_ON_BOOT, DB_POOL_MAX,
# METRICS_ENABLED, TRACING_SPAN_PROCESSOR, JOBS_PROVIDER, API_PORT and NODE_ENV.
# NODE_ENV=production
#
# Three keys are set by the stack itself and should not be overridden here, because
# the value has to agree with a resource CDK created: SECRETS_AWS_SECRET_ID,
# STORAGE_S3_BUCKET / CACHE_DYNAMODB_TABLE, and SECURITY_TRUSTED_CLIENT_IP_HEADER
# (which the rate limiter keys on, and which only CloudFront can populate).

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bringing your own infrastructure — mostly NOT configured from this file
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pointing the platform at infrastructure you already run is a *construct library*
# concern, not an env one: you write your own version of bin/grant.ts and pass
# `vpc: IVpc`, `storage.uploadsBucket: IBucket`, `cache.table: ITable`, an
# ICertificate or an IHostedZone. See docs/deployment/aws-serverless.md
# § Bring your own infrastructure, and ADR 0005.
#
# Two cases people reach for this file to solve, and what is actually true:
#
#   DB_URL — does NOT work yet. The props say you may omit the `database` prop and
#   supply DB_URL here, but the same condition also gates the API function, which
#   reads DB_URL from the platform secret the stack creates alongside its own
#   cluster. Omit `database` today and you get no serving function. Recorded in
#   lib/grant-platform.ts as a follow-up; until it lands this target runs the Aurora
#   cluster it creates.
#
#   REDIS_* with CACHE_STRATEGY=redis — honoured by the application, but the stack
#   generates no route to a cluster it did not create. You would supply the VPC, the
#   security-group rule and the cluster, and a DynamoDB table is still created unless
#   you also pass `cache.table`. Config alone is not enough here.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets — never synthesized; written to the platform secret after deploy
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Applied with:  pnpm --filter grant-aws-deploy put-secrets
#
# ORIGIN_VERIFY_SECRET is deliberately absent: the stack generates it and CloudFront
# is configured with it. Setting it here is refused.

# OAuth app client secret, from the same GitHub app as GITHUB_CLIENT_ID above.
GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Encrypts stored TOTP secrets. Generate once; losing it invalidates every
# enrolled authenticator.
#   openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_MFA_SECRET_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
